# Security Analysis

## Threat Model

The GOAD-Cloud system has two distinct security concerns:

1. **The lab is intentionally vulnerable.** The Windows Domain Controller and workstation are configured with deliberate misconfigurations for the security scanner to detect. They must never be reachable from the internet.

2. **The cloud platform itself must be secure.** The dashboard, API, and data stores must be protected from misuse, tampering, and data leakage even though the underlying scan target is vulnerable by design.

## Network Security

The vulnerable AD lab lives entirely within a VPC. The Windows DC and workstation have no public IPs. The Linux scanner has a public IP only for SSM bootstrapping; its security group denies all inbound traffic.

The dashboard is a static site on S3, served over HTTPS via the S3 endpoint. API Gateway terminates TLS for all API calls. No HTTP traffic is permitted anywhere.

## Identity and Access Management

Each component runs under its own IAM role:

| Component | Permissions |
|-----------|-------------|
| Trigger Lambda | Write DynamoDB; call SSM SendCommand on the scanner instance ARN only |
| Scanner EC2 | Read DynamoDB; write the raw S3 bucket only; CloudWatch logs |
| Report Lambda | Read raw S3 bucket; write reports S3 bucket; update DynamoDB |
| Status Lambda | Read DynamoDB only |
| Download Lambda | Read reports S3 bucket; sts:GetCallerIdentity for presigned URL signing |

No human users have console access to the AWS account beyond the project team. No long-lived access keys are issued.

## Data Protection

- All S3 buckets use SSE-S3 encryption at rest
- DynamoDB uses AWS-managed encryption at rest
- API Gateway and S3 enforce HTTPS in transit
- Presigned URLs for PDF downloads expire after 15 minutes
- Raw findings are deleted after 30 days via S3 lifecycle policy

## Audit and Monitoring

CloudTrail logs every management API call to a dedicated S3 bucket. CloudWatch collects Lambda execution logs and EC2 system logs. A CloudWatch alarm fires if more than five scans fail in any one-hour window.

## Risk Mitigations

| Risk | Mitigation |
|------|------------|
| AD credentials in source code | Credentials are read from EC2 environment variables, never committed to Git |
| Scanner EC2 compromised | Has write-only access to one S3 prefix; cannot read other tenants or pivot to the AD lab beyond LDAP |
| Public dashboard discoverable | The dashboard URL is shared only with authorized users; in production this would sit behind authentication |
| Report PDFs leaked | Presigned URLs expire after 15 minutes; bucket is otherwise private |
| AWS account billing abuse | CloudWatch billing alarm fires at $20 spend |

## Known Limitations

This is a student academic project, not production software. Several production-grade controls are deliberately out of scope:

- No multi-factor authentication on the dashboard
- No user accounts or per-user authorization
- No rate limiting on the API
- No customer-managed KMS keys (AWS-managed keys only)
- No automated incident response

These would all be added in a real deployment.